1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:02,280
You like living in New York, don't you?

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:03,350
Oh, I love it.

3
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:04,790
It's so convenient.

4
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:06,210
I can take the bus to work ...

5
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:07,020
or the subway ...

6
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:08,180
or a taxi.

7
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:10,360
And there's so much to do.

8
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,330
Lots of movie houses, and the theater.

9
00:00:13,330 --> 00:00:15,130
I know what you mean.

10
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:16,910
I'd like to live in the city,

11
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:18,820
but living in New Jersey and the suburbs

12
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:19,930
is better for Michelle.

13
00:00:19,930 --> 00:00:22,420
Trees, grass.

14
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:25,450
There's a lot of good things about suburban living.

15
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:27,590
I grew up in Riverdale, remember?

16
00:00:27,590 --> 00:00:28,690
So I know.

17
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:31,280
But, as a working woman,

18
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,280
I think New York has all the conveniences--

19
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:39,950
including the best tomatoes.

20
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:43,000
The truth is, I'd like to live in the city.

21
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,130
Michelle's the right age.

22
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:47,630
There are lots of things for her here.

23
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:48,780
You're right, Harry.

24
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:50,630
Today is the perfect example.

25
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:53,710
Michelle and her friends are at the aquarium in Brooklyn.

26
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:55,420
They come back here for lunch,

27
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:58,130
then go uptown to the Museum of Natural History.

28
00:00:58,130 --> 00:01:00,450
There's so much for young people to see and do.

29
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:01,820
It's just incredible!

30
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:02,940
Not just for young people.

31
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:04,070
What about me?

32
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:05,520
I've never been to the aquarium

33
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:06,920
or the Museum of Natural History.

34
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:07,700
Have you?

35
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:08,780
Oh yes, Harry.

36
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:09,760
My mother and father

37
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,200
often took us somewhere in the city on the weekends.

38
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:13,490
Dad was a busy doctor,

39
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:15,910
but he usually managed to squeeze a Sunday in

40
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:17,400
with Richard, Robbie, and me.

41
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,740
I used to love to go to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

42
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:22,010
I've been there several times.

43
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:24,420
Twice with Michelle.

44
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:26,760
You ought to think about spending more time with Michelle

45
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:29,450
at all the great places in the city.

46
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:32,130
Well, perhaps,

47
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:35,450
you'll help me select some of the great places.

48
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:40,340
And perhaps, you'll join us?

49
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:47,440
Perhaps I will.

50
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:48,860
Well, there we are.

51
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:50,480
All set for lunch, Harry.

52
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:51,510
It looks inviting.

53
00:01:51,510 --> 00:01:53,090
I wish Michelle and her friends would get here.

54
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:55,150
I'm starving, aren't you?

55
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:57,790
I can't wait to take a bite of the pumpernickel.

56
00:01:57,790 --> 00:02:00,430
It smells so delicious.

57
00:02:00,430 --> 00:02:05,290
Coming! Coming!

58
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:06,170
Who is it?

59
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:07,910
Michelle.

60
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:09,240
It's us, Susan.

61
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:10,150
Come in, Michelle,

62
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:11,370
and bring your friends along.

63
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:12,670
We're on the fifth floor.

64
00:02:12,670 --> 00:02:18,840
Oh, you've been here before.

65
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,360
Susan,

66
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,830
I really appreciate

67
00:02:23,830 --> 00:02:25,720
your doing this for Michelle and her friends.

68
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:26,770
Oh, please, Harry.

69
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:28,760
It's nothing.

70
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:31,470
I'm not just doing it for Michelle.

71
00:02:31,470 --> 00:02:34,730
I'm doing it for you.

72
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:37,710
Thank you.

73
00:02:37,710 --> 00:02:43,140
I'm doing it for us, Harry.

74
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:43,750
Well,

75
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:47,010
it's ... it's important for Michelle to see us together more often.

76
00:02:47,010 --> 00:02:48,710
That's true.

77
00:02:48,710 --> 00:02:49,580
It's important

78
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:53,170
for Michelle and me to get to know each other better.

79
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:58,390
That's important for us.

80
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:00,980
That makes me feel good.

81
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:02,160
What, Harry?

82
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:03,470
What makes you feel good?

83
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:05,050
Well, that you care about Michelle,

84
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:06,610
that you care about me, and

85
00:03:06,610 --> 00:03:11,170
that you care about us.

86
00:03:11,170 --> 00:03:14,620
Well, Harry, that's because I do.

87
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:19,570
I do care.

88
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:21,040
They'll be here any minute.

89
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:28,000
Susan, I'd like to continue this conversation later.

90
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,680
Of course, Harry.

91
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:33,710
We'll finish the conversation when they go to the museum.

92
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:35,820
I'd like that.

93
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:38,820
So would I.
